2FA Simple Bypass – PortSwigger Lab Write-Up

This repository contains my write-up for the “2FA Simple Bypass” lab from the PortSwigger Web Security Academy.

This lab demonstrates how Two-Factor Authentication (2FA) can be bypassed when it is not properly enforced on the server side.

Lab Goal

The goal of this lab was to access Carlos’s account without completing the 2FA step.

Given Credentials

Attacker account
wiener : peter

Victim account
carlos : montoya

Step 1: Login with Attacker Account

Logged in using wiener:peter

Username and password were accepted

Application requested a 2FA code

Entered the valid 2FA code received via email

Login was successful

After login, the account page URL was:

/my-account

Step 2: Login with Victim Account

Logged out and logged in using carlos:montoya

Username and password were accepted

Application asked for a 2FA code

No access to Carlos’s email, so the 2FA code could not be entered

Step 3: Bypass 2FA

While still on the 2FA verification page

Manually changed the URL to:

/my-account

The page loaded successfully

Access to Carlos’s account was granted without completing 2FA

Lab solved.

Why Did This Work?

This issue occurred because:

The account page was not properly protected

The application did not verify whether 2FA was completed

There was no proper server-side validation

This is an example of a Broken Access Control vulnerability.

What I Learned

2FA must always be enforced on the server side

Client-side or UI-based checks are not sufficient

Sensitive endpoints must verify authentication status

Small implementation mistakes can lead to serious security issues

Conclusion

This lab shows that security vulnerabilities are not always complex.
Sometimes, missing validation checks are enough to break application security.

PortSwigger labs are very useful for understanding real-world web security issues.

